  {
    "id": "m02-choice-01",
    "type": "choice",
    "title": "Classroom situations",
    "prompt": "The pupils are looking at the teacher with their textbooks closed. The teacher wants them to open the books to page 6.",
    "options": [
      "Hold your books up.",
      "Open your books at page 6.",
      "Turn to page 75.",
      "Put your hands down."
    ],
    "answer": 1,
    "explanation": "Only “Open your books at page 6” instructs pupils to open the textbook at the specified page. “Hold your books up” means lift the books; the last option identifies a different page. “Put your hands down” concerns raised hands, not textbooks."
  },
  {
    "id": "m02-choice-02",
    "type": "choice",
    "title": "Classroom situations",
    "prompt": "All pupils are already ready to repeat a word. The teacher wants them to begin speaking at the same moment rather than take individual turns.",
    "options": [
      "Next one, please.",
      "Whose turn is it to read?",
      "All together now.",
      "A full sentence, please."
    ],
    "answer": 2,
    "explanation": "“All together now” is a cue for simultaneous speaking. “Next one, please” advances to the following turn; “Whose turn is it to read?” asks who reads next. “A full sentence, please” requests the form of an individual answer, not simultaneous repetition."
  },
  {
    "id": "m02-choice-03",
    "type": "choice",
    "title": "Classroom situations",
    "prompt": "A pupil gives the correct idea but answers using only one word. The teacher wants a grammatically complete response.",
    "options": [
      "A full sentence, please.",
      "Louder, please!",
      "Have another try!",
      "Whose turn is it to read?"
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "explanation": "The issue is the FORM of the answer, not its volume or correctness. “A full sentence, please” asks for a complete sentence. “Whose turn is it to read?” asks about turn-taking, not answer structure."
  },
  {
    "id": "m02-choice-04",
    "type": "choice",
    "title": "Classroom situations",
    "prompt": "The teacher is midway through a difficult explanation and wants to check that the students can follow the reasoning.",
    "options": [
      "Are you ready?",
      "Have a go!",
      "Do you follow me?",
      "Who knows the answer?"
    ],
    "answer": 2,
    "explanation": "“Do you follow me?” explicitly checks whether the students are following the explanation. “Are you ready?” asks about readiness, and “Have a go!” encourages an attempt. “Who knows the answer?” invites a response; it does not check whether the current explanation is understood."
  },
  {
    "id": "m02-choice-05",
    "type": "choice",
    "title": "Classroom situations",
    "prompt": "A pupil enters late. Rather than guessing the cause, the teacher asks where the pupil has been.",
    "options": [
      "Where have you been?",
      "Did you oversleep?",
      "Did you miss your bus?",
      "Any questions?"
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "explanation": "“Where have you been?” asks about the pupil’s whereabouts without assuming a reason. The other two options suggest particular causes of lateness. “Any questions?” invites questions; it does not ask where the late pupil has been."
  },
  {
    "id": "m02-choice-06",
    "type": "choice",
    "title": "Classroom situations",
    "prompt": "A pupil has just finished an assigned presentation successfully. The teacher wants to praise the completed work.",
    "options": [
      "Well done.",
      "You’ve improved a lot.",
      "Have another try!",
      "Unfortunately not."
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "explanation": "“Well done” praises this completed task. “You’ve improved a lot” compares progress over time, and “Have another try!” asks for a new attempt. “Unfortunately not” rejects an answer rather than praising completed work."
  },
  {
    "id": "m02-choice-07",
    "type": "choice",
    "title": "Classroom situations",
    "prompt": "A student gives an answer that is nearly right. The teacher wants them to make another attempt.",
    "options": [
      "That’s it!",
      "Congratulations!",
      "Almost. Try again.",
      "You’re on the right lines."
    ],
    "answer": 2,
    "explanation": "“Almost. Try again.” recognises a nearly correct answer and directly invites another attempt. “That’s it!” confirms success, while “Congratulations!” celebrates an achievement. “You’re on the right lines” suggests a promising approach, but does not explicitly request a fresh attempt after a nearly correct answer."
  },
  {
    "id": "m02-choice-08",
    "type": "choice",
    "title": "Classroom situations",
    "prompt": "The teacher is going into the corridor for one minute and promises the class that they will return.",
    "options": [
      "I’ve got to go next door for a moment.",
      "I’ll be back in a moment.",
      "There’s no hurry.",
      "Come in."
    ],
    "answer": 1,
    "explanation": "“I’ll be back in a moment” is the only option that directly PROMISES a return. Going next door describes the destination; “There’s no hurry” concerns time pressure. “Come in” permits someone to enter, not promises that the teacher will return."
  },
  {
    "id": "m02-choice-09",
    "type": "choice",
    "title": "Classroom situations",
    "prompt": "A pupil asks the teacher for permission to sit down because they feel dizzy.",
    "options": [
      "Sit down.",
      "Do you mind if I sit down?",
      "Are you better now?",
      "Are you ready?"
    ],
    "answer": 1,
    "explanation": "“Do you mind if I sit down?” politely requests permission. “Sit down” is an instruction; “Are you better now?” asks about health. “Are you ready?” checks readiness, not permission to sit."
  },
  {
    "id": "m02-choice-10",
    "type": "choice",
    "title": "Classroom situations",
    "prompt": "Two pupils are chatting while the teacher is giving instructions. The teacher asks them to stop the conversation.",
    "options": [
      "Look this way.",
      "Stop talking.",
      "Listen to what Anna is saying.",
      "Put your hands down."
    ],
    "answer": 1,
    "explanation": "“Stop talking” specifically asks pupils to end the conversation. The others redirect attention but do not explicitly ask them to stop speaking. “Put your hands down” is about raised hands, not conversations."
  },
  {
    "id": "m02-choice-11",
    "type": "choice",
    "title": "Classroom situations",
    "prompt": "A pupil’s answer is correct, but classmates at the back cannot hear it. The teacher asks for greater volume.",
    "options": [
      "A full sentence, please.",
      "All together now.",
      "Louder, please!",
      "Have another try!"
    ],
    "answer": 2,
    "explanation": "“Louder, please!” addresses volume. A full sentence concerns structure; “All together now” cues a group response. “Have another try!” asks for a repeat attempt, not louder speech."
  },
  {
    "id": "m02-choice-12",
    "type": "choice",
    "title": "Classroom situations",
    "prompt": "The teacher wants a pupil to come to the board and write the solution, not just approach the front.",
    "options": [
      "Come out and write it on the board.",
      "Come to the front of the class.",
      "Look this way.",
      "Hold your books up."
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "explanation": "Only “Come out and write it on the board” asks for both movement and writing. Coming to the front omits the writing step. “Hold your books up” asks pupils to raise textbooks rather than write on the board."
  },
  {
    "id": "m02-choice-13",
    "type": "choice",
    "title": "Classroom situations",
    "prompt": "Compared with earlier lessons, a pupil’s pronunciation has become noticeably better. The teacher comments on progress over time.",
    "options": [
      "That’s very good.",
      "You did a great job.",
      "You’ve improved a lot.",
      "Have a go!"
    ],
    "answer": 2,
    "explanation": "“You’ve improved a lot” refers to change over time. The other responses praise an answer or completed task without explicitly referring to progress. “Have a go!” encourages a first attempt, not improvement over time."
  },
  {
    "id": "m02-choice-14",
    "type": "choice",
    "title": "Classroom situations",
    "prompt": "A pupil is thinking carefully about a difficult question. The teacher reassures them that they may take their time.",
    "options": [
      "Next one, please.",
      "Have another try!",
      "There’s no hurry.",
      "Be careful."
    ],
    "answer": 2,
    "explanation": "“There’s no hurry” removes time pressure. The other options advance to another person or ask for another attempt. “Be careful” advises caution, not freedom from time pressure."
  },
  {
    "id": "m02-choice-15",
    "type": "choice",
    "title": "Classroom situations",
    "prompt": "After showing an example, the teacher wants to ask informally whether pupils understand it.",
    "options": [
      "Do you get it?",
      "Are you ready?",
      "Who knows the answer?",
      "Come to the front of the class."
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "explanation": "“Do you get it?” checks comprehension. “Are you ready?” asks about readiness, and “Who knows the answer?” invites a person to answer a question. “Come to the front of the class” gives a movement instruction and does not ask about understanding."
  },
  {
    "id": "m02-choice-16",
    "type": "choice",
    "title": "Classroom situations",
    "prompt": "For a demonstration, one particular pupil must leave their desk and stand at the front, without writing anything.",
    "options": [
      "Stand by your desks.",
      "Come to the front of the class.",
      "Come out and write it on the board.",
      "Hold your pens up."
    ],
    "answer": 1,
    "explanation": "“Come to the front of the class” requests movement to the front only. The other options ask for a different position or an additional writing action. “Hold your pens up” is about displaying pens rather than moving to the front."
  },
  {
    "id": "m02-choice-17",
    "type": "choice",
    "title": "Classroom situations",
    "prompt": "Pupils have already completed the first two steps. The teacher introduces an activity that must happen AFTER those steps.",
    "options": [
      "First of all, ...",
      "For the last thing today, let’s ...",
      "After that, ...",
      "Whose turn is it to read?"
    ],
    "answer": 2,
    "explanation": "“After that” explicitly connects a following step to what has already happened. “First of all” begins a sequence; “For the last thing today” introduces the final lesson activity. “Whose turn is it to read?” asks about whose reading turn comes next, not the next stage of the activity."
  },
  {
    "id": "m02-choice-18",
    "type": "choice",
    "title": "Classroom situations",
    "prompt": "Pupils are taking turns reading aloud. The teacher wants to identify whose turn it is next.",
    "options": [
      "Whose turn is it to read?",
      "Who knows the answer?",
      "Next one, please.",
      "Any questions?"
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "explanation": "“Whose turn is it to read?” asks WHICH pupil should read. “Next one, please” asks the next person to proceed without asking who it is. “Any questions?” invites questions about the material rather than identifying the next reader."
  },
  {
    "id": "m02-choice-19",
    "type": "choice",
    "title": "Classroom situations",
    "prompt": "A pupil voluntarily helps the teacher carry a heavy box. The teacher wants to thank the pupil specifically for being considerate.",
    "options": [
      "You did a great job.",
      "That really is very kind of you.",
      "Well done.",
      "Congratulations!"
    ],
    "answer": 1,
    "explanation": "“That really is very kind of you” recognises a thoughtful act. The other phrases praise how well a task was performed. “Congratulations!” celebrates an achievement, but does not specifically acknowledge the pupil’s considerate act."
  },
  {
    "id": "m02-choice-20",
    "type": "choice",
    "title": "Classroom situations",
    "prompt": "A pupil asks if there will be time for a game. The teacher cannot give a fixed answer because it depends on finishing the exercise.",
    "options": [
      "Unfortunately not.",
      "There’s no hurry.",
      "It depends.",
      "Yes, that’s right."
    ],
    "answer": 2,
    "explanation": "“It depends” expresses a conditional answer. “Unfortunately not” is a definite refusal, while “There’s no hurry” reassures somebody about time. “Yes, that’s right” confirms a correct statement, but does not express dependence on a condition."
  },
  {
    "id": "m02-choice-21",
    "type": "choice",
    "title": "Classroom situations",
    "prompt": "A pupil returns to school after being absent yesterday due to illness. The teacher wants to ask explicitly whether they feel better TODAY.",
    "options": [
      "Do you feel better today?",
      "Did you miss your bus?",
      "Are you ready?",
      "Where have you been?"
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "explanation": "“Do you feel better today?” asks about wellbeing and contrasts today with the previous illness. The other options ask about lateness or readiness. “Where have you been?” asks about whereabouts, not recovery from illness."
  },
